index c1d9469e7284a576f2803e20d95c6922cdcc1f3a..6046d31a69055a749e1685383937d21bcbb5182c 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -12,3 +12,30 @@ CLI: lineexport INPUT OUTPUT [LIMIT]. Success is exit 0 without stdout; failure
 exit 1 with stderr and no success message. Invalid usage exits 2. Accepted prefix
 may already exist on failure; no atomic-publication promise. Paths are positional,
 including names beginning with -. No external dependency is needed.
+
+## Usage
+
+```sh
+lineexport input.txt output.txt       # all records
+lineexport input.txt output.txt 0     # all records
+lineexport input.txt output.txt 10    # at most ten records
+lineexport -input.txt -output.txt 2   # paths beginning with '-' are positional
+```
+
+LIMIT is an optional nonnegative integer. A malformed, negative, or overflowing
+limit is invalid usage (exit 2), checked before opening or truncating files.
+Successful exports produce no stdout or stderr. Read, write, open, and writer
+finalization failures exit 1 and print the error to stderr. Output is created or
+truncated on a valid invocation; a failure can leave a partially accepted prefix.
+
+Library callers can use `Export(r, w)` for unlimited output or
+`ExportLimit(r, w, limit)` for a prefix. Both return the number of fully written
+records and close the owned writer exactly once, even when the limit is invalid.
+They leave the borrowed reader open. Empty lines and a final record without a
+newline count; output records end in a newline. Existing Scanner behavior is
+preserved, including CRLF normalization and the default token size limit.
+
+A single read, write, or Close failure is returned as the original error, so
+direct equality continues to work. When copying and Close independently fail,
+use `errors.Is` or `errors.As` to inspect either cause. A short write with no error
+is reported as `io.ErrShortWrite` and does not count as a fully accepted record.
